  "attribution": "Copyright © 1991-2026 Unicode, Inc. Emoji keywords and names from the Unicode Common Locale Data Repository (CLDR) 49, common/annotations/ and common/annotationsDerived/: en.xml for the app, and a pack language's files for its pack, distributed under the Unicode License v3.",
  "verdict": "use",
  "verdict_reason": "Permissive, like the Unicode emoji data: the CLDR data files are under the Unicode License v3, which allows modified copies with the notice, carried in the app's About screen.",
  "used_for": "Emoji suggestions in the bar: the one-word keywords and names (tts) of each emoji, lowercased, compiled by data/pipeline/emoji_data.py into emoji-words.txt; and the emoji panel's search: every word of each name and keyword, into emoji-search.txt, with the derived names (flags by country, keycaps, people by role and gender) from annotationsDerived. English's files are the app's (apps/ios/KeyboardExtension/Emoji/); a pack language's (\"packs\" here: pack id to CLDR locale, its files <locale>.xml and derived-<locale>.xml) go to data/langs/<id>/ by data/build-emoji.sh and into its pack as the emojiWords and emojiSearch roles, and Malayalam's also to the app's Emoji/packs/ml/, for a Malayalam pack published before packs carried them. Native script only: CLDR has no romanised words (no Manglish, no Hinglish). Not here: Bhojpuri, which CLDR 49 has no annotation files for (bho.xml is a 404), and Maithili, whose mai.xml names 8 emoji and whose derived file one; their packs bring no emoji words, and Hindi's are not put in their place.",
